   libheif and libde265 are C/C++ libraries. Here they are compiled to
   JavaScript with Emscripten (an asm.js build; the file contains no
   WebAssembly), with libheif's Emscripten bindings (build-emscripten.sh,
   pre.js, post.js and libheif/heif_emscripten.h in its repository), and
   that build runs inside the Web Worker heic2any starts from a blob: URL.
   heic2any's copy of that build is src/libheif.js in its repository at
   tag 0.0.4; its embedded program data is byte-identical to the one inside
   heic2any.min.js.

3. gifshot, MIT License, Copyright 2017 Yahoo Inc.
   Builds animated GIFs; heic2any only uses it for GIF output, which
   Orisod's HEIC to JPG tool never asks for.
   License: LICENSE-gifshot (LICENSE.txt from the npm package gifshot@0.4.5;
   gifshot's GitHub repository, github.com/yahoo/gifshot, is no longer online)
   heic2any.min.js doesn't state which gifshot version it holds. It is a
   re-minified copy of gifshot's minified build, closest to gifshot 0.4.5
   (the latest release on npm); the exact version can't be confirmed.

How the versions were found:
- libheif 1.10.0: called inside heic2any's worker in a browser,
  heif_get_version() returns "1.10.0" and heif_get_version_number() gives
  1.10.0 as well. The string "1.10.0" is also stored in the library's program
  data inside heic2any.min.js, next to libheif's own messages.
- libde265 1.0.2: libheif names its decoder "libde265 HEVC decoder, version "
  followed by de265_get_version(), and the program data holds
  "libde265 HEVC decoder", ", version " and "1.0.2" in that order.
  libheif's build-emscripten.sh at v1.10.0 also downloads and builds exactly
  libde265 1.0.2 (LIBDE265_VERSION=1.0.2). The functions this build exposes to
  JavaScript (heif_get_version, heif_context_alloc, heif_js_decode_image and
  a few more) don't include one that returns libde265's version, so this
  one comes from the file's contents and that build script, not from
  running it.

Replacing it: Orisod uses heic2any.min.js unmodified and loads it as its own
file, /assets/vendor/heic2any/0.0.4/heic2any.min.js, at the end of the HEIC to
JPG page (/heic-to-jpg/ and /es/heic-to-jpg/). Nothing else on the site
depends on its contents. You can build libheif, libde265 and heic2any
yourself from the sources above (with your own changes, if you like) and use
your build in its place, for example with your browser's local overrides
feature, which serves your own file at that address.
